              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_2",
              "text": "It was constructed in 1929 and was designed by architect Emery Roth.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_3",
              "text": "The Beresford is 22 stories tall and is topped by octagonal towers on its northeast, southwest, and southeast corners.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_4",
              "text": "The building is a contributing property to the Central Park West Historic District, a National Register of Historic Places–listed district, and is a New York City designated landmark.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_5",
              "text": "By the late 1920s, high-rise apartment buildings were being developed on Central Park West in anticipation of the completion of the New York City Subway's Eighth Avenue Line, which opened in 1932.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_6",
              "text": "Central Park West was concurrently widened from 48 to 63 feet (15 to 19 m).",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_7",
              "text": "Under the Multiple Dwelling Act of 1929, this allowed the construction of proportionally taller buildings on the avenue.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_8",
              "text": "The Beresford, with its three relatively short towers, had been designed just before the passage of the act.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_9",
              "text": "It contrasted with the multi-story twin towers of the Century, the Majestic, the San Remo, and the El Dorado, which were all built one to two years after the Beresford was completed.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_10",
              "text": "As Christopher Gray of The New York Times wrote: \"Had the Beresford been designed a year later, its three towers would have sprouted up like Jack's beanstalk.\"",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_11",
              "text": "The Beresford Central Park West Corporation, headed by Bennett Gordon, acquired the old Hotel Beresford from Frederick Brown in March 1925.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_12",
              "text": "Sugarman and Berger filed plans the next year for a high-rise apartment building to replace the hotel.",
              "source_title": "Wikipedia: The Beresford (section: History)",
              "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1370202541#History",
              "source_revised_at": "2026-08-19T18:38:32Z",
              "source_kind": "encyclopedia"
            },
            {
              "id": "claim_13",
              "text": "The LPC building database lists 211 Central Park West (aka 1-9 West 81st Street; 2-12 West 82nd Street) (The Beresford): architect/builder Emery Roth; primary style neo-Renaissance; date 1928 - 1929; historic district Upper West Side / Central Park West Historic District.",
              "source_title": "NYC LPC: Individual Landmark and Historic District Building Database (NYC Open Data)",
              "source_url": "https://data.cityofnewyork.us/d/gpmc-yuvp",
              "source_revised_at": null,
              "source_kind": "official_record"
            }
          ],
